# Shell scripts must check out with LF endings, on every platform.
#
# OPS-02: found by rehearsing infra/rollback.sh on a Windows checkout. git had no
# attributes for it at all -- `git check-attr text eol -- infra/rollback.sh` reported
# "unspecified" for both -- so with core.autocrlf the file arrives with CRLF and bash reads
# its first functional line, `set -euo pipefail`, as `pipefail\r`:
#
#     infra/rollback.sh: line 24: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# The script then runs on with none of -e, -u or -o pipefail in force, which is the worse
# half of the problem: an incident tool that keeps going after a failed command instead of
# stopping. Every `.sh` in this repository had the same exposure.
#
# This matters specifically for the INCIDENT scripts (infra/rollback.sh, infra/deploy.sh,
# infra/alb.sh): whoever is on call may be on Windows, and "the rollback script will not
# start" is not something to discover during an outage. git already stores LF, so this
# changes only what lands in a working tree -- Linux and CI are unaffected.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the other files executed by an interpreter that treats CR as content.
*.bash text eol=lf
*.zsh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts keep CRLF, for the mirror-image reason.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
